<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Massylii</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 202 BCE (approx.) · capital Cirta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern Numidian kingdom of Gaia and Masinissa, whose cavalry helped decide the Second Punic War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Massylii were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> people of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, bordering Carthaginian territory. By the 3rd century BCE they were ruled by kings, and they long fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> as allies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>, supplying its famous Numidian cavalry.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom was a federation of tribes under a royal clan, with succession often passing between brothers, which caused disputes. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaia-numidian-king">Gaia</a> sent his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> to fight for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. After Gaia's death the throne passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oezalces">Oezalces</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capussa">Capussa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacumazes">Lacumazes</a> amid civil war, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/masaesyli">Masaesyli</a> seized Massylian lands. Masinissa switched sides to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 206 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With Roman help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a> recovered his father's kingdom, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a> in 203 BCE and fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Zama</a> in 202 BCE. He then united the Massylii and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/masaesyli">Masaesyli</a> into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/numidia">Numidia</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a> as its capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/massylii.jpg" alt="Massylii" width="320" height="166"><figcaption>Image: Bibliothèque nationale de France, département Monnaies, médailles et antiques, Luynes 3958 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tridrachme,_Argent,_Numidie,_Micipsa2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 202 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cirta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gaia, Masinissa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Berber religion, Punic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Numidian (Libyco-Berber), Punic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>, <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of the Massylii, Eastern Numidia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-251&amp;focus=massylii" aria-label="Massylii on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4846 866 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Massylii lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5166 1122L5259 1106M5166 1122L5166 1122"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5259 1106h0M5166 1122h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5166 1122h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5166 1122h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cirta. <a href="/atlas?year=-251&amp;focus=massylii">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
